ca in 2016\" is enough to support that at the time of the Forbes Africa feature he had 67 albums? Let's consider if a human reading would infer that. \"67 albums later, Mtukudzi still spoke as if... telling Forbes Africa in 2016...\" The \"later\" is relative to his album production. It says after 67 albums, he still spoke... telling Forbes Africa in 2016. That indicates by the time of the 2016 Forbes Africa interview, he had 67 albums. However, the phrase \"67 albums later\" could be at the time of the article (2019) and \"telling Forbes Africa in 2016\" is an example. The grammar: \"67 albums later, Mtukudzi still spoke as if he was in search of what to call a career, telling Forbes Africa in 2016, '...'\" The main verb \"spoke\" is not necessarily tied to 2016? The participle \"telling Forbes Africa in 2016\" is an adverbial phrase specifying the occasion of speaking. So \"still spoke ... telling Forbes Africa in 2016\" means in 2016 he spoke. The \"67 albums later\" is a temporal frame for that speaking. Thus it does suggest that at that 2016 speaking, he had 67 albums later? Actually \"later\" is not a state; it's a point after 67 albums. It says after 67 albums, he still spoke... telling Forbes Africa in 2016. So yes, by 2016 he had 67 albums.\n\nBut is \"67 albums later\" an exact count at 2016? Could he have had 67 albums at time of article (2019) and in 2016 he had fewer? The sentence's temporal ordering: 67 albums later (after those albums) -> he spoke (in 2016). If the albums were produced after 2016, then \"67 albums later\" would be after 2016, but then \"telling Forbes Africa in 2016\" would be before the 67 albums, contradicting \"later\". If \"later\" is relative to something else, not necessarily the speaking. For instance, \"67 albums later [i.e., by now], Mtukudzi still spoke as if... [as he did] telling Forbes Africa in 2016...\" This reading places the 67 albums at article time, and the 2016 quote as an earlier example. That is grammatically pla